  <h2 id="sales">Who pays for these skills, and how they judge you</h2>

  <p>
    Employers and clients pay for clear thinking that leads to decisions. They judge you by the logic of your case and the honesty about risks.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first basic skill is a one-page case with a question, facts, options and a recommendation.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">AI and business strategy careers: which tasks are under pressure</h2>

  <p>
    AI tools summarise markets and draft frameworks. Generic strategy slides become common.
  </p>
  <p>
    Choosing the right question, testing the weakest assumption and owning the recommendation hold value. A staged path helps: now, use AI for research drafts; later, own the judgment.
  </p>

  <h2 id="better">Alternatives that can beat a general business strategy careers path</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Alternative</th>
          <th>Why it can beat it</th>
          <th>Catch</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Consulting route</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Fast learning</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Long hours</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Product route</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Closer to building</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Different skills</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Startup route</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Broad scope</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Uncertain pay</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="learn">How to learn business strategy careers cheaply, and what proof counts</h2>

  <p>
    Business strategy careers is learned by doing real work, so the cheapest route is also the best. A paid course is worth it only when it gives feedback and finished work that you cannot get free.
  </p>
  <h3>Free and low-cost routes first</h3>
  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>Free YouTube lessons and public company reports.</li>
    <li>Free NPTEL and SWAYAM management and statistics courses.</li>
    <li>Free spreadsheet practice with public data.</li>
    <li>A professional in the role who reviews your sample.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>How to judge a paid course</h3>
  <p>
    Judge each course, not the platform. Ask what you will build, who reviews it, which role graduates reached, how current the material is and what the refund terms are, in writing.
  </p>
  <h3>Your week, and a first small test</h3>
  <p>
    A starting plan is about ten hours a week: three of lessons and seven of practice on one project. Do not pay for an expensive programme before this habit holds.
  </p>
  <p>
    In four weeks, write two one-page cases on real companies and ask a professional to find the weakest assumption.
  </p>
  <h3>Building a high-income skill portfolio beside the credential</h3>
  <p>
    A degree has a place, and the weak spot is relying on it alone. A skill mix of one core skill, a second skill, communication, a niche and a clear offer builds a high-income skill portfolio, and a niche service can bring an earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

  <h2 id="where">Where strategy work sits</h2>

  <p>
    Strategy is a function, not one job. These are common homes.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Home</th>
          <th>Typical work</th>
          <th>Entry route</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Home"><strong>Corporate strategy team</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Typical work">Market, competitor and growth studies</td>
          <td data-label="Entry route">Experience in the firm or a strong postgraduate route</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Home"><strong>Consulting firm</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Typical work">Client problem studies and recommendations</td>
          <td data-label="Entry route">Campus hiring or strong lateral entry</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Home"><strong>Startup</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Typical work">Growth plans and positioning</td>
          <td data-label="Entry route">Early role with broad scope</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Home"><strong>Government and policy units</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Typical work">Planning and policy notes</td>
          <td data-label="Entry route">Public exams or research roles</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Entry rules differ widely, so read the actual notices.
  </p>

  <h2 id="case">A written case that shows strategy skill</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Section</th>
          <th>What to write</th>
          <th>Check</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Section"><strong>Question</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What to write">One clear question about a real company</td>
          <td data-label="Check">Can it be answered with evidence?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Section"><strong>Facts</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What to write">Three to five checkable facts from public sources</td>
          <td data-label="Check">Are sources named?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Section"><strong>Options</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What to write">Two or three choices with trade-offs</td>
          <td data-label="Check">Is each option fair?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Section"><strong>Recommendation</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What to write">One choice and why</td>
          <td data-label="Check">Does it follow from the facts?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Section"><strong>Risks</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What to write">What could make it wrong</td>
          <td data-label="Check">Is the weakest assumption stated?</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="sources">Where to find honest evidence for a strategy case</h2>

  <p>
    A case is only as good as its facts. Prefer primary, public and checkable sources.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Source type</th>
          <th>Good for</th>
          <th>Caution</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Source type"><strong>Company annual reports and filings</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Good for">Revenue, costs and risks stated by the firm</td>
          <td data-label="Caution">Read the notes, not only the summary</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Source type"><strong>Regulator and government data</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Good for">Market rules and sector totals</td>
          <td data-label="Caution">Check the date of each table</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Source type"><strong>Industry association reports</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Good for">Sector trends</td>
          <td data-label="Caution">Members may promote their sector</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Source type"><strong>News and interviews</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Good for">Recent events and claims</td>
          <td data-label="Caution">Verify with a second source</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Source type"><strong>Customer conversations</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Good for">What buyers say they need</td>
          <td data-label="Caution">Small samples, so state the limit</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Name every source in your case and note what each fact does and does not prove.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pitfalls">Five reasoning errors that weaken a recommendation</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Error</th>
          <th>What it looks like</th>
          <th>Fix</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Error"><strong>Starting with the answer</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What it looks like">Facts are chosen to fit</td>
          <td data-label="Fix">Write the question first</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Error"><strong>Ignoring the base case</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What it looks like">No comparison to doing nothing</td>
          <td data-label="Fix">Add a do-nothing option</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Error"><strong>Single-point forecasts</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What it looks like">One number with no range</td>
          <td data-label="Fix">Show best, likely and worst</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Error"><strong>Copying a competitor</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What it looks like">Assuming their path suits you</td>
          <td data-label="Fix">Ask what is different about you</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Error"><strong>Hiding the weakest link</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What it looks like">Risks are buried</td>
          <td data-label="Fix">State the weakest assumption first</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="money">Money: course fees and the 10 percent test</h2>

  <p>
    As a rule of thumb and not a research finding, keep a degree or course fee near a tenth of the family's total education budget, and treat spending above roughly a fifth as a serious caution that needs course-specific evidence.
  </p>
  <p>
    An illustration, not a real fee: on a Rs 8 lakh education budget, a tenth is Rs 80,000 for the main course, and a fifth is Rs 1.6 lakh as a serious caution. The rest can fund a laptop, practice time and a reserve.
  </p>
  <p>
    Test the field with free courses and one finished project before paying for a long programme. Do not borrow heavily for a course whose graduates cannot show placement in a specific role.
  </p>

